nodecode-wslc is a rewrite of Yicheng-Mark's Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins:

  https://github.com/Yicheng-Mark/hermes-windows-plugins
  commit 5bad3fb2baf04ce6befecfefce6d14f4c9c98934
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Yicheng-Mark (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's wslc CLI adapter and its config shapers, in Common
Lisp. hermes_wslc_sandbox/wslc.py's contract is what cli.lisp implements: the
binary resolution order (an explicit path, PATH, then the default Windows
install location), `ps` read as NDJSON and `inspect` as an array, existence
decided by the exit status because the CLI localizes its prose, the
`run --help` probe that decides which hardening flags this build takes instead
of assuming Docker's parity, and the container name and label rules from
config.py (a persistent sandbox's name reproducible, a non-persistent one
unique per run, only a name this cell started its own to remove). Its shape is
one agent-facing tool, wslc_exec, two more over the same CLI, and eval verbs,
instead of Hermes's terminal environment provider.

Left out, and why: Hermes's terminal-environment provider itself -- the
BaseEnvironment subclass, its cwd markers, stdin plumbing and process handles
-- is not rewritten, because Nodecode's own shell is its own and a cell
cannot replace it; the sandbox is reached through the tool and the verbs
instead. For the same reason the container is created on the first command
rather than at gateway boot, so a machine without the CLI is not refused at
start. `container_disk` is reported by the original and enforced by neither
(it was only ever a note: wslc has no `--storage-opt`), so this cell does not
carry the setting at all; the HERMES_WSLC_BINARY override is the `binary`
setting here. The plugin's private core imports have no counterpart: nothing
here reaches into Nodecode's shell.
